  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Confidence continues for a period of torrential and \na risk of flooding across much of Rhode Island and southeast \nMassachusetts. \n\nMain forecast focus for the next 48 hours continues to be on a \nshortwave and warm front later tonight through much of the day on \nSunday. Aloft, the area will find itself in the right entrance \nregion of a 80-90 kt jet by 12z Sunday morning. So, synoptically, \nthere are plenty of jet and frontal dynamics at play to allow for a \nheavy rain event. The thermodynamic environment certainly isn't \nlacking either, with model soundings showing PWATs climbing to >2\" \nwhich is near the 99th percentile of climatology for September 13th. \nThese highly anomalous PWATs pair with a nearly saturated \natmospheric column and warm cloud heights of 12-14kft and allow for \nefficient rainfall processes and areas of torrential rain. Taking \nthese conditions into account it makes sense that the 12z HREF shows \na 40-55% chance for 3 hour rainfall amounts to exceed flash flood \nguidance covering an area from eastern Connecticut to Rhode Island \nand SE Massachusetts. Additionally, there is still a strong (55-70%) \nchance for 6-24 HR precipitation values to exceed the 5 year ARI and \na moderate (25-40%) chance of exceeding the 10 year ARI. The \ngreatest concern for areas of flash flooding is across the urban \ncorridor from Providence to interior SE MA. Still some uncertainty \nwith regards to the exact placement of the heaviest rain it will be \ncontingent upon the placement of a mesoscale low. Subtle \ndifferences in deterministic guidance have this band as far south as \nthe south coast and as far north as northern Massachusetts. Think \nthe HREF and RRFS handle these differences best by centering \nthe highest rainfall totals along the I-95 corridor. \n\nGiven the uncertainty and the N jog in guidance, opted to expand the \nFlood Watch to include Windham county in eastern Connecticut, \nsouthern Worcester County, Norfolk county, and Suffolk County \nincluding Boston. Elsewhere, a widespread 0.50\" to 1.50\" expected \nacross much of our region Within the watch there will likely be \nlocalized narrow bands of 2\"-4\" of rain possible inside 3 hours. The \nnarrow but intense area of rainfall likely comes to an end from west \nto east between 2 and 5 pm.\n\nOn a final note, while the main threat is areas of flash flooding, \nthere is a low albeit non-zero chance for isolated severe weather \nacross coastal RI and MA. CAMs show a narrow area of MLCAPE \napproaching 300-500 J/kg across coastal areas. Paired with 0-1km \nshear of ~20kts and curved hodographs there is a very low chance for \na spin up or a waterspout as the mesolow passes late tomorrow \nmorning into the afternoon. What's lacking is adequate instability \nor lapse rates for deeper convection but worth keeping an eye on. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure in control next week brings generally \nquiet weather. Brief bout of summer warmth and humidity expected Wed-\nFri.\n\nThe start of the new work week starts with some lingering showers as \nthe cold front makes its way offshore. Rain comes to an end after \nsunrise for western MA/CT while showers linger into the early-mid \nafternoon across eastern MA/RI. Eventually dry NW post frontal flow \nadvects 40-50F dewpoints overhead which combined with highs in the \nlow to mid 70s Mon and Tue will make for a comfortable and \nseasonable first half of the week. This, as high pressure is in \ncontrol at the surface through much of the week keeping us dry. As \nthe high shifts offshore Wednesday flow turns southerly bringing the \nreturn of more summer-like temperatures and dewpoints in the upper \n70s and mid 60s respectively. Uncertainty increases with regard to \nthe forecast for late week next weekend. Zonal mid level flow \ncontinues into the weekend with potential for a low to cross \nsomewhere in the vicinity of southern New England; whether that \nstays to our north or goes overhead is uncertain, but generally \nlooking at a better shot of unsettled weather late Thursday\nonward.",
